WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.500
Film Courage: Do you have any suggestions
to help writers brainstorm?

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:05.310
Scott Myers, DePaul University Professor/Screenwriter/Creator
Gointothestory.blcklst(dot)com: Yes, well

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:12.019
first of all let's imagine that we've got
10 tracks in our brain (that our brain can

00:00:12.019 --> 00:00:15.690
do 10 things at one time).

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:23.131
One of those tracks if you're a writer should
be always sensitive to and aware of is ideas

00:00:23.131 --> 00:00:27.250
that are out there all over the place.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:31.710
They are in newspaper articles, they are in
conversations you hear with people at a coffee

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:39.769
shop, they are on the radio, they are on the
click, click, click thing.

00:00:39.769 --> 00:00:46.030
I know (for example) David Rabinowitz and
Charlie Wachtel who wrote BlacKkKlansman I

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.719
interviewed them and said well how did you
guys come up with it and I think David said

00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:54.999
Well I was trying to avoid writing so I was
doing the click, click, click thing and I

00:00:54.999 --> 00:00:59.710
stumbled on this Facebook article about this
guy Ron Stallworth, the first black police

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:03.710
officer in Colorado Springs, so when they
did this thing with the Ku Klux Clan so that’s

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:05.320
how they discovered it.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.720
One thing is just be conscious of it.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.160
The other thing is be intentional.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:17.640
Like everyday you should be thinking about
story ideas.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:24.020
Now people are not going to necessarily, it’s
going to vary from person-to-person but there

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:27.990
have been times in my life where I’ve literally
said I’m going to come up with an idea a

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.430
day (every day) I’m going to come up with
something.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:36.000
And a third point to that related is this
Dr. Linus Pauling who was the only person

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.210
to win two Nobel prizes and he has that saying
“The best way to come up with a good idea

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.710
is to come up with a lot of ideas.”

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:49.560
So that’s a third point that you can do
is generate a lot of ideas, keep a file like

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.850
I’ve got a Word file stretching back to
20 some odd years ago with literally hundreds

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:56.220
and hundreds ideas (most of them awful).

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:02.811
But you keep working that muscle and then
eventually I think chances are that you are

00:02:02.811 --> 00:02:04.730
going to surface something.

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:09.269
Now then you have to assess the ideas and
so there are certain questions you have to

00:02:09.269 --> 00:02:13.801
ask like “Well is this big enough to be
a movie?

00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:15.569
Who is the audience for this?

00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:18.390
What is the hook?

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.249
That central conceit.”

00:02:20.249 --> 00:02:28.340
When I sold K-9 I remember going into an executives
office and they said “What you guys did

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.180
was so brilliant.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:37.060
You took the buddy action comedy formula (the
police cop) and you put in a Rin Tin Tin.”

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:41.551
Who was like the biggest star in Hollywood
from back in the late 1920’s and 1930’s.

00:02:41.551 --> 00:02:46.010
I learned a very valuable lesson there which
is you always when somebody gives you a compliment

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:50.260
even if you didn’t mean it you go “Yes,
that’s exactly what I was thinking!”

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:58.840
And it wasn’t that at all but that idea
I could walk in at any point in time in 1987,

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:02.930
I could walk into any producer’s office
or any bar where people are hanging out and

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:04.010
“What are you working on?”

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:06.579
“Yes, I’ve got this idea for K-9.”

00:03:06.579 --> 00:03:09.959
Loner cop, new partner and a police dog.

00:03:09.959 --> 00:03:11.090
They would see that.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.749
So the hook there is the police dog.

00:03:13.749 --> 00:03:15.639
Nobody had done that you know.

00:03:15.639 --> 00:03:20.290
So you look for like INCEPTION, the idea to
be able to go inside someone’s mind.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:21.290
GROUNDHOG DAY, we are living that same thing.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:26.939
What is that thing that is going to set it
off from something else.

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:27.939
Those are some keys.

00:03:27.939 --> 00:03:35.680
You have one part of your brain going be intentional
about it, be generating a lot of ideas and

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.359
hoping that you will come up with some really
good ones, some strong ones off of that and

00:03:39.359 --> 00:03:42.849
then assess them to make sure that you feel
like it’s big enough to be a movie.

